{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310804,"nonce":"h+6Tm1+3BCqua++IXUed0ja2wyM=","title":"MEASURED 2026-08-07: the Q4 band kernel is SPILL-bound (663 of 3923 insns touch the stack, ~4 per vpdpbusd, 872-byte frame) — not glue-, ...","message":"mov 769 · add 260 · vpdpbusd 168 · lea 163 · and 162 · shr 140\n    movslq 137 · imul 133 · vpaddd 128 · vpmulld 104 · or 103 · vpand 98\n    shl 97 · vgf2p8affineqb 92 · vpbroadcastd 74\n\n    STACK TRAFFIC: 663 insns touch (%rsp) — 360 loads, 289 stores\n    frame = 0x368 (872 B).  ~4 stack accesses per vpdpbusd.\n\n`mov` at 19.6% is the largest category and it is mostly SPILL, not data\nmovement. That is the real shape of the kernel — the earlier labels\n(\"glue-bound ~94%\", \"52% address arithmetic\") both described the instruction\nMIX rather than the limiter.\n\nHypothesis: `q4DotBody` decodes 16 scale/min values up front and holds 8 `dv`\nvectors before combining, so live ranges exhaust 16 GPRs. Remedy: compute\n`imin` immediately after the header decode (kills the 8 `mn` early) and fold\neach sub-block dot into one of 4 accumulators as produced (keeps 1 `dv` live,\npreserving the old (0,4)(1,5)(2,6)(3,7) ILP pairing). Integer adds are exact\nand associative, so bit-identity holds by construction.\n\n**Result: spills went UP.** 663 -\u003e 695 stack insns, body 3923 -\u003e 3939, and\nwall clock 3/3 pairs SLOWER (+0.7% to +5.4%). REVERTED.\n\n**Source-level live-range shaping does not steer this register allocator.** It\nwas already scheduling the wide version better than my hand ordering. Do not\nretry reordering variants of this function; if spills are to fall it has to\ncome from the compiler side (allocation/rematerialisation) or from needing\nless state at once (a genuinely different kernel shape).\n\n1. f32/`Single` scales — 4/4 pairs SLOWER ([[perf_single_f32_q4_dot_no_win]])\n2. cache blocking — `NUE_NROW_BLOCK` 16..256 moves ttft 4.4%, under the ~8%\n   noise floor. (af630eeb's ORIGINAL blocking win stands; further TUNING is\n   dead — and this was already recorded as \"insensitive\", I re-derived it.\n   READ THE PERF FILE BEFORE RE-MEASURING.)\n3. scalar index math — removed 29% of sign-extends, zero time change\n   ([[perf_prefill_gap_is_address_arithmetic]], theory CLOSED 8a56c5d9)\n4. live-range restructure — this file\n\n`perf stat -e cpu_core/cycles/,cpu_core/instructions/` on the whole prefill run:\n\n    baseline   305.0G cyc / 994.9G ins -\u003e IPC 3.26   (band_ms path)\n               310.1G cyc / 1023.4G ins -\u003e IPC 3.30\n\n**IPC 3.26 on Alder Lake P-cores is HIGH.** The kernel is NOT stalled, NOT\nlatency-bound, NOT memory-bound — it is retiring near its issue width. So\n**time is proportional to DYNAMIC instruction count**, and the only lever that\ncan work is doing the same math in fewer instructions (wider vectors / more\noutputs per pass), not removing stalls.\n\n**perf is also a 3x better instrument than wall time here**: instruction counts\nrepeat within ~2.9% run-to-run where band_ms drifts 8-10%. **Use instruction\ncount as the A/B metric on this box, not seconds.**\n\nThe \"delete the 6-bit unpack\" arm below executed **MORE** work, not less:\n\n    baseline  994.9G / 1023.4G instructions\n    noglue   1099.6G / 1092.0G instructions   (+7-10%), cycles +10%\n\nFeeding garbage scale/min values produced pathological FP (denormals/NaN -\u003e\nmicrocode assists), so the arm measured numerics, not the header decode.\n**\"Removing the header decode buys nothing\" is WITHDRAWN — its dynamic cost is\nstill UNKNOWN.** To size it properly, use an arm that is numerically sane\n(e.g. read pre-decoded scales from a real side table) and diff INSTRUCTION\nCOUNTS, not seconds.\n\nFourth control-was-broken instance this session (cf. the `-ot` repack flag, the\ncold page cache, the stale NUC binary). **A probe that changes VALUES is not a\nprobe of COST.**\n\nBefore building a pre-decoded scale table, I bounded what it could buy:\nreplaced the whole 6-bit scale/min unpack with plain byte reads (WRONG results,\nthrowaway arm) — deleting ~502 insns, **12.8% of the body**.\n\n    band_ms  bs 6366.6 / 6695.3 / 7004.9 / 6884.3\n             noglue 6415.6 / 6927.3 / 6796.2 / 7079.6   -\u003e 3/4 SLOWER, mixed\n\n**Removing an eighth of the instructions changed nothing measurable.** So a\npre-decoded scale side-table (the obvious repack-lite) is NOT worth building,\nand neither is any further instruction-count reduction. Combined with the\nsign-extend result (-29%, no change), the kernel's time is not in issuing\ninstructions — suspect memory/latency or a dependency chain (candidate:\n104 `vpmulld`, ~10-cycle latency, applying the sub-block scales).\n\n`NUE_PROFILE_POOL=1` gives `band_ms` directly (plus per-worker ms and chunk\ncounts; workers balance within 2.3%, so it is not load imbalance). Scoping to\nband_ms removes load/quant/generation — but it does NOT improve resolution:\nband_ms drifts ~8-10% run-to-run on the NUC (6366 -\u003e 7005 across four runs of\nthe SAME arm, monotonic = thermal). **Every source-level effect available here\nis 1-5%, i.e. under the noise.** Five A/Bs this session all landed inside it.\n\n**Hardware counters would settle it in one run** (cycles/IPC/stalls/misses says\ndirectly whether it is issue-bound, latency-bound or memory-bound) — but the\nNUC has `kernel.perf_event_paranoid = 4` and no passwordless sudo, so `perf`\nis unusable. UNBLOCK: `sudo sysctl kernel.perf_event_paranoid=1` on the NUC\n(persist in /etc/sysctl.conf). Until then, do not attempt more few-% kernel\nA/Bs here — they cannot be validated.\n\n- **Repack / multi-row**: llama.cpp's remaining edge is interleaved weights\n  feeding wide loads across 8 rows at once — one weight load and one header\n  decode amortised over 8 outputs. That reduces the STATE per output, which is\n  the one thing the census says we are short of. Measured +41.5% for them\n  ([[perf_repack_confirmed_x86_prefill_but_not_first]]); untested on ours.\n  NOTE that file argues repack would underdeliver here because the loop is\n  \"glue-bound (~4% vpand/vpor)\" — that sizing predates this census and should\n  not be trusted as a reason NOT to try it.\n- **Compiler-side**: 663 stack ops for 168 VNNI ops is an allocation outcome,\n  not a Haxe-source outcome. This is the concrete form the \"Haxe at Rust\n  parity\" directive takes for this kernel.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_q4_kernel_is_spill_bound_not_source_tunable` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}